  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.099,
            0.104,
            0.216,
            0.121
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "POLYNOMIALS"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.875,
            0.105,
            0.898,
            0.119
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "11"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.144,
            0.901,
            0.189
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "a cubic polynomial are "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "2-x^{3},x^{3},\\sqrt{2}x^{3},3-x^{2}+x^{3},3x^{3}-2x^{2}+x-1"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . In fact, the most general form of a cubic polynomial is"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.403,
            0.195,
            0.595,
            0.213
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_1_equation_3.jpg",
          "content": "a x ^ { 3 } + b x ^ { 2 } + c x + d ,"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.22,
            0.528,
            0.238
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "where, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "a ,   b ,   c ,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " d are real numbers and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "a \\neq 0"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.244,
            0.902,
            0.323
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Now consider the polynomial "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "p ( x ) = x ^ { 2 } - 3 x - 4"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . Then, putting "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x = 2"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " in the polynomial, we get "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "p(2)=2^{2}-3\\times 2-4=-6"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . The value "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "^ { \\circ } - 6 ^ { \\circ }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , obtained by replacing x by 2 in "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x ^ { 2 } - 3 x - 4"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , is the value of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x ^ { 2 }   - 3 x   - 4"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " at "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x = 2"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . Similarly, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "p ( 0 )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is the value of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "p ( x )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " at "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x = 0"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , which is "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "^ { - 4 }"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.328,
            0.899,
            0.367
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "If p(x) is a polynomial in x, and if k is any real number, then the value obtained by replacing x by k in "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "p ( x )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , is called the value of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "p ( x )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " at "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x = k"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , and is denoted by "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "p ( k )"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.147,
            0.372,
            0.723,
            0.391
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "What is the value of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "p ( x ) = x ^ { 2 } - 3 x - 4"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " at "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x = - 1 ?"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " We have :"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.26,
            0.397,
            0.592,
            0.417
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_1_equation_8.jpg",
          "content": "p(-1)=(-1)^{2}-\\{3\\times(-1)\\}-4=0"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.422,
            0.525,
            0.441
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Also, note that "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "p ( 4 ) = 4 ^ { 2 } - ( 3 \\times 4 ) - 4 = 0 ."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.447,
            0.901,
            0.506
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "As "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "p ( - 1 ) = 0"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "p ( 4 ) = 0 , - 1"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and 4 are called the zeroes of the quadratic polynomial "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x ^ { 2 } - 3 x - 4"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . More generally, a real number k is said to be a "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "zero of a",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " polynomial "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "p ( x )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , if "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "p ( k ) = 0"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.511,
            0.901,
            0.586
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "You have already studied in Class IX, how to find the zeroes of a linear polynomial. For example, if k is a zero of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "p(x) = 2x + 3"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , then "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "p ( k ) = 0"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " gives us "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "2 k + 3 = 0"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , i.e., "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "k = - \\frac { 3 } { 2 } ."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.584,
            0.895,
            0.665
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In general, if k is a zero of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "p(x) = ax + b."
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , then "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "p ( k ) = a k + b = 0 ,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " i.e., "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "k = { \\frac { - b } { a } }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " So, the zero of the linear polynomial "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "a x + b"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac{-b}{a} = \\frac{- ( Constant term )}{ Conflictint   x}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.669,
            0.901,
            0.732
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Thus, the zero of a linear polynomial is related to its coefficients. Does this happen in the case of other polynomials too? For example, are the zeroes of a quadratic polynomial also related to its coefficients?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.739,
            0.9,
            0.781
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In this chapter, we will try to answer these questions. We will also study the division algorithm for polynomials."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.796,
            0.702,
            0.816
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2.2 Geometrical Meaning of the Zeroes of a Polynomial"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.824,
            0.902,
            0.902
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "You know that a real number k is a zero of the polynomial "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "p ( x )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " if "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "p ( k ) = 0"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . But why are the zeroes of a polynomial so important? To answer this, first we will see the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "geometrical",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " representations of linear and quadratic polynomials and the geometrical meaning of their zeroes."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.435,
            0.966,
            0.563,
            0.983
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
